    <h1>Build a Wasm provider</h1>
    <p>Add a command to Dekopon’s controlled shell runtime. Declare typed arguments and capabilities in Rust; the SDK exports a component that streams bytes, while the broker authorizes each call and bounds its I/O.</p>
    <nav class="sdk-jumps" aria-label="Provider guide">
      <a href="#provider-map">The boundary</a>
      <a href="#interfaces">Available I/O</a>
      <a href="#start">Working example</a>
    </nav>
  </header>

  <section id="provider-map" aria-labelledby="map-heading">
    <h2 id="map-heading">Your code inside the broker</h2>
    <figure class="sdk-map" aria-labelledby="map-caption">
      <div class="sdk-map-entry">
        <strong>Agent’s bash script</strong>
        <code>probe upper --text hello</code>
        <span>Command word routes to your component ↓</span>
      </div>
      <div class="sdk-map-broker" data-sdk-boundary="broker">
        <p class="sdk-map-label">Broker process · supported imports linked at load</p>
        <div class="sdk-map-guest" data-sdk-boundary="guest">
          <h3>Your Wasm component</h3>
          <dl>
            <div><dt><code>describe()</code></dt><dd>The SDK derives command words, capabilities and input schemas from your types. Metadata isn’t permission.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>run_command()</code></dt><dd>Clap parses argv; your <code>Provider::propose</code> selects a capability. It sees whether stdin exists, never its bytes, and calls no host imports.</dd></div>
            <div><dt><code>invoke()</code></dt><dd>The SDK validates JSON input and calls <code>Capability::run</code> with typed needs and stdout. Your code writes bytes and returns success or a typed failure.</dd></div>
          </dl>
        </div>
        <div class="sdk-map-policy" data-sdk-boundary="policy">
          <h3>Operator configuration</h3>
          <p>Caller mapping + Cedar policy + execution constraints</p>
          <p class="sdk-map-edge"><code>run_command()</code> → policy decision<br>Allow → <code>invoke()</code><br>Deny → no invocation</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sdk-map-host" data-sdk-boundary="host">
          <h3>Broker host services</h3>
          <p>↑ Input streams and handles · ↓ output bytes and effect requests</p>
          <p>HTTP destinations, storage scope and asset operations use the invocation’s authority. Credentials are resolved and injected only by the native host.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sdk-map-secret" data-sdk-boundary="credential">
          <h3>Broker-held credentials</h3>
          <p>Native HTTP injects these.<br>Never passed to your Wasm code</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption id="map-caption">The component imports <code>{{ providerSdk.interfaces.stdio }}</code>. It receives invocation-scoped streams and handles, never host paths or broker credentials.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <p class="sdk-limit"><strong>The boundary has limits.</strong> A permitted endpoint receives its credential; transformed reflection is outside the protection. Wasmtime runs inside the broker, not a separate OS process. A compromised broker host defeats this boundary. <a href="{{ source }}/docs/security-model.md">Security model ↗</a></p>
    <p class="sdk-note">Cedar evaluates static rules, not intent. A granted capability can still be misused within its constraints. <a href="/guides/access/">Configure identity and access</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="lifecycle" aria-labelledby="lifecycle-heading">
    <h2 id="lifecycle-heading">Each invocation has fresh memory and byte streams.</h2>
    <p><code>{{ providerSdk.interfaces.provider }}</code> exports <code>describe</code>, <code>run-command</code> and <code>invoke</code>. Invocation output goes to stdout; the export returns an exit status, not a JSON result envelope. JSON is a format your command can write, including its newline. Old <code>provider@0.3.0</code> components are refused.</p>
    <p>Compiled code can be reused, but each invocation gets a fresh store and instance with fuel, memory and work-time limits. Stdio backpressure and child waits park the work clock; the shared shell deadline still applies. A closed downstream reader stops the producer with status <code>141</code>.</p>
    <p>Direct storage writes and completed HTTP effects survive a later trap or timeout. There’s no invocation rollback. <a href="/guides/runtime/#limits">Runtime limits</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="interfaces" aria-labelledby="interfaces-heading">
    <h2 id="interfaces-heading">Typed needs select the host interfaces.</h2>
    <p>Each <code>Capability</code> declares <code>Input</code>, <code>Needs</code> and <code>Error</code>. Its <code>run(input, needs, out)</code> writes to <code>Stdout</code>; <code>stdin()</code> returns an optional byte reader. A declared import never widens the broker’s grants.</p>
    <div class="sdk-table-scroll" role="region" aria-label="Provider interfaces" tabindex="0">
      <table class="sdk-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Need</th><th scope="col">What the broker supplies</th><th scope="col">Contract / example</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Compute / stdio</th><td data-label="What the broker supplies"><code>Needs = ()</code> needs no effect handles; stdio remains imported. Closed input schemas and <code>Bounded&lt;N&gt;</code> reject unknown fields and oversized text when declared.</td><td data-label="Contract / example"><a href="{{ example }}/src/lib.rs">CLI probe ↗</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">HTTP</th><td data-label="What the broker supplies"><code>Http</code>: buffered <code>send</code> or asset-backed <code>stream</code> through <code>{{ providerSdk.interfaces.http }}</code>. Destination/method and byte limits still apply. No HTTP + storage in one capability.</td><td data-label="Contract / example"><a href="{{ source }}/examples/providers/http-probe/src/lib.rs">HTTP probe ↗</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Signed downloads</th><td data-label="What the broker supplies"><code>signed_download::download</code> reads a bounded GitHub Actions log window through an explicit <code>signedDownload: github-actions-logs-v1</code> HTTP constraint. The host follows the validated API redirect; the signed URL and API credential stay outside guest code.</td><td data-label="Contract / example"><a href="{{ source }}/crates/dekopon-provider-sdk/src/signed_download.rs">Signed-download API ↗</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Storage / settings</th><td data-label="What the broker supplies"><code>Storage&lt;Jsonl&gt;</code> or <code>Storage&lt;DurableFiles&gt;</code> uses <code>dekopon:storage@0.1.1</code> inside its namespace and quotas. <code>Settings&lt;T&gt;</code> reads <code>providerSettings.&lt;provider-id&gt;</code>, never secret values; use <code>provider::endpoint::Base</code> for an owner-selectable API origin.</td><td data-label="Contract / example"><a href="{{ source }}/examples/providers/storage-probe/src/lib.rs">Storage probe ↗</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Assets</th><td data-label="What the broker supplies"><code>Assets</code> opens invocation-bound references. Attach, remove and send need their respective grants; attaching does not send. <a href="/guides/runtime/#assets">File ceilings</a>.</td><td data-label="Contract / example"><a href="{{ source }}/crates/dekopon-provider-sdk/src/asset.rs">Asset API ↗</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Time / entropy</th><td data-label="What the broker supplies"><code>Clock</code> and <code>Monotonic</code> use <code>dekopon:clock@1.1.0</code> for wall time and elapsed nanoseconds, including child waits; <code>Random</code> reads OS entropy in chunks of at most 4096 bytes. Invoke only; no separate effect grant.</td><td data-label="Contract / example"><a href="{{ source }}/examples/providers/clock-probe/src/lib.rs">Clock probe ↗</a></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Child scripts</th><td data-label="What the broker supplies"><code>Spawn</code> asks the gateway to run one child at a time under the caller’s identity and shared tree budget. Every nested capability is authorized again.</td><td data-label="Contract / example"><a href="{{ source }}/crates/dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit/tests/spawn.rs">Native / component tests ↗</a></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="sdk-note">No general WASI, arbitrary host processes or raw sockets. The broker refuses unsupported imports, including HTTP <code>client@1.2.0</code>; use the linked <code>client@1.1.0</code> stream API. The separate signed-download interface is <code>dekopon:http/signed-download@0.1.0</code>, not a replacement HTTP client.</p>
    <p><code>Spawn::run(script, ChildStdin)</code> accepts no input, inherited input or an explicit reader. After <code>Inherit</code>, stop reading the parent’s stdin: the child shares its remaining bytes and may read ahead. Child stdout is bounded script text emitted at exit; <code>wait()</code> discards unread output and returns status. An ordinary child has no separate stderr stream.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading">
    <h2 id="start-heading">The CLI probe is a complete typed provider.</h2>
    <p><a href="{{ example }}/src/lib.rs"><strong>CLI probe ↗</strong></a> declares <code>Provider</code> with clap-derived arguments and three <code>Capability</code> types, then calls <code>dekopon_provider_sdk::export!(CliProbe)</code>. The command supports help, piped input and text transforms. Start here instead of assembling raw WIT bindings.</p>
    <div class="sdk-start-grid">
      <div>
        <h3>Build the pinned example</h3>
        <p>Requires Git, rustup and <code>wasm-tools {{ providerSdk.versions.wasmTools }}</code>. The checkout pins Rust {{ providerSdk.versions.rust }} and its dependencies. Run from the repository root:</p>
        <pre aria-label="Clone, test and build the example" tabindex="0"><code>git clone --depth 1 --branch {{ release.tag }} \
  https://github.com/dekopon-agents/dekopon.git \
  dekopon-provider-lab
cd dekopon-provider-lab
rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown

cargo test --locked \
  --manifest-path examples/providers/cli-probe/Cargo.toml
cargo build --locked --release --target wasm32-unknown-unknown \
  --manifest-path examples/providers/cli-probe/Cargo.toml
wasm-tools component new \
  examples/providers/cli-probe/target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/dekopon_cli_probe_provider.wasm \
  -o examples/providers/cli-probe-provider.wasm</code></pre>
        <p class="sdk-note">This is a local development build. A Mac provider build that links zstd also needs <code>AR_wasm32_unknown_unknown</code> set to LLVM’s <code>llvm-ar</code>; the system archiver can produce an empty Wasm archive. <a href="{{ example }}/build.sh">The release build script ↗</a> additionally normalizes build metadata and paths.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Check streams and the component contract</h3>
        <pre aria-label="Inspect and test the component" tabindex="0"><code>wasm-tools validate examples/providers/cli-probe-provider.wasm
wasm-tools component wit examples/providers/cli-probe-provider.wasm
cargo test --locked -p dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit \
  --test typed conformance_accepts_checked_cli_probe
cargo test --locked -p dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit \
  --test typed real_component_captures_piped_bytes_and_status
cargo test --locked -p dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit \
  --test typed closed_real_stdout_reader_terminates_producer_early</code></pre>
        <ul class="sdk-checks">
          <li><strong>Native tests:</strong> help and typed dispatch, closed schemas, bounded text and invalid arguments.</li>
          <li><strong>Decoded WIT:</strong> three exports and the <code>{{ providerSdk.interfaces.stdio }}</code> import. <code>run-command</code> takes argv plus a boolean; <code>invoke</code> returns <code>result&lt;_, u8&gt;</code>.</li>
          <li><strong>Real host tests:</strong> piped <code>piped bytes\n</code> produces <code>{"text":"PIPED BYTES\n"}</code> plus a newline at status 0; empty input gives status 2; closed stdout gives 141.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="sdk-limit"><strong>Not a policy test.</strong> The testkit grants test authority without Cedar. <code>Native&lt;P&gt;</code> injects ports; <code>Harness&lt;P&gt;</code> executes the component in the real broker host. Neither proves your production grants. <a href="{{ source }}/crates/dekopon-provider-sdk-testkit/tests/typed.rs">Test source ↗</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p><code>dekopon_provider_sdk::skills!("my-provider/SKILL.md")</code> can embed text from the crate’s <code>skills/</code> directory. Export the validated files offline with <code>dekopon-brokerd provider --lock-file LOCK --store STORE skills --out DIR</code>, then mount them in the gateway catalog. Skill text grants no authority; a component’s embedded instructions do not install themselves.</p>
    <p><strong>Make it yours:</strong> change the provider identity, clap argument tree and capability implementations in <code>src/lib.rs</code>. Keep <code>export!(P)</code>; the broker requires declared command words and a callable <code>run-command</code>. In an independent repo, replace the path dependency with <code>dekopon-provider-sdk = "={{ release.version }}"</code>. Clap is already re-exported; no feature flag is needed. <a href="https://crates.io/crates/dekopon-provider-sdk/{{ release.version }}">Published SDK ↗</a>.</p>
  </section>

